<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cardigan Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cardigan Bay is a large bay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llyn-peninsula">Llŷn Peninsula</a> in the north to Strumble Head in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a>. Its coast includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a>, Aberaeron and Barmouth, and its waters are known for bottlenose dolphins. Welsh legend tells of a drowned kingdom, Cantre'r Gwaelod, beneath the bay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cardigan-bay.jpg" alt="Cardigan Bay" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Graham Well, Bath, England · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardigan_bay_graham_well.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cardigan Bay, in Welsh Bae Ceredigion, is a large inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> that indents the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> between Bardsey Island in Gwynedd in the north and Strumble Head in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a> in the south. It is the largest bay in Wales. Much of the coast around it is fertile farmland, dotted with towns and seaside resorts including Fishguard, New Quay, Aberaeron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdyfi">Aberdyfi</a>, Tywyn, Barmouth, Porthmadog, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criccieth">Criccieth</a> and Pwllheli. Many rivers flow into the bay, among them the Glaslyn, Mawddach, Dysynni, Dyfi, Rheidol, Ystwyth, Aeron and Teifi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until the early 20th century Cardigan Bay supported a strong maritime industry. Cardigan lies at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-teifi">River Teifi</a>, hence its Welsh name Aberteifi, meaning mouth of the Teifi. At the turn of the 19th century, the heyday of its port, more than 300 ships were registered there, seven times as many as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> and three times as many as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swansea">Swansea</a>.</p><p>The central and northern parts of the bay are the site of the Cantre'r Gwaelod of Welsh legend, the drowned Lowland Hundred or Hundred under the Sea. A forest near Borth, which 4,500 years ago stretched over boggy land as far as Ynyslas, was buried under layers of peat, sand and saltwater as sea levels rose. In the winter storms of 2014 parts of it reappeared on the shoreline as preserved tree stumps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation protects the wildlife of the sea and the foreshore. The bay's main marine mammals are harbour porpoises, grey seals and bottlenose dolphins, of which it has the largest population in the UK, and minke whales, Risso's dolphins, common dolphins, puffins and basking sharks may also be seen. A military testing range was first set up in the bay during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Controlled from a base near Aberporth, it has played a part in developing and testing weapons, and its danger area covers some 6,500 square kilometres of the bay.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cardigan Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom covered a mountainous coastal region including Cadair Idris, with farming on the valley floors and the coast, and its people were linked by sea with the rest of <b>Cardigan Bay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-meirionnydd">Kingdom of Meirionnydd</a></li></ul></section>
